sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/darwin1859-ch12-11> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Somewhat independently of distance, the depth of sea separating an island from the neighbouring mainland is related to whether they share the same or allied mammal species: islands separated by shallow channels have the same or very similar mammals, those across deep water have distinct species and genera.";
    dc:type "empirical-generalization";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Sea depth between island and mainland predicts their mammalian affinity";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Charles Darwin, On the Origin of Species (1st ed., London: John Murray, 1859), Chapter XII, “Geographical Distribution, continued”; section “Absence of Batrachians and of terrestrial Mammals”. Scope: terrestrial mammals of continental and near-continental islands"
}
